  "query_preview": "..\nToday: A strengthening high pressure over central Conus will start \nnudge a ridge into the Pacific Northwest. It will start a dry, \nwarming trend for the region that will extend into the much of next \nweek. Lingering residual moisture and instability will bring weak \nconvection during the afternoon and early evening hours. Best \nchances (10-20%) will be over the mountain areas of the region. \nBasin is not expected to see much in the showers or thunderstorms. \nHighs will be a few degrees warmer than yesterday with less cloud \ncover expected. Temperatures expected to reach into the 80s to low \n90s. \n\nMonday through Thursday: With the ridge expected through the much of \nthe week, any chances of precip will diminish through the period. \nThe only chances will come from weak waves moving North to South \nalong a deep trough East of the Continental Divide. Ensembles are \nshowing very little moisture associated with these waves as the clip \nNortheast WA and the Idaho Panhandle. Temperatures will continue to \nthe climb a degree or two each day. Highs will be in the upper 80s \nto 90s. \n\nFriday and Saturday: Pattern change expected by the end of the week, \nas ridge slides East and a Low pushes into the Pacific Northwest. It \nis still fairly early to highlight potential impacts. For now, it \nwill lead to increased precip chances mainly over the mountains. \nEnsembles do have winds increasing on Saturday which could \nbring an elevated fire concern. Temperatures will be on a cooler\ntrend as Saturdays highs will be in the 80s. /JDC",
